  "generated_description": "<div class=\"mountain-description prose prose-stone max-w-none\">\n  <p><strong>Pu\u02bbukeonehehe\u02bbe</strong> is a 11,529-foot summit in Hawaii. The Pu\u02bbukeonehehe\u02bbe Trail covers 4.7 miles with about 3,891 feet of elevation gain \u2014 a steep, sustained climb. It's rated Strenuous overall.</p>\n  <h3>Getting There</h3><p>The trailhead is at <a href=\"https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=19.81014,-155.468\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">19.81014, -155.468</a> (tap for driving directions). Arrive early on weekends \u2014 trailhead lots at popular peaks fill quickly, and cell service is often unreliable once you leave the highway.</p>\n  <h3>When to Go</h3><p>The reliable snow-free window is roughly July through September. At this elevation, afternoon thunderstorms are a serious summer hazard \u2014 start early and plan to be off the upper mountain by midday. Outside that window expect snow travel and bring traction and navigation skills to match.</p>\n  <h3>Know Before You Go</h3><p>The Strenuous rating comes from the National Park Service difficulty formula, which weighs total distance against elevation gain. Most hikers should budget about 3.5 hours of moving time; add margin for breaks, photos and the descent. Download the GPX track from this page before you go \u2014 cell coverage is unreliable in the mountains and a saved route works offline in any GPS app. Conditions change: verify current access, closures and weather with the official land manager before you go. Trail data is sourced from OpenStreetMap contributors (ODbL).</p>\n  <p>Looking to link objectives or plan a weekend? Nearby summits include Pu\u02bbuko\u02bboko\u02bbolau, Pu\u02bbul\u012bl\u012bnoe and Pu\u02bbuwaiau \u2014 each has its own guide on this site.</p>\n</div>",
